You previously flagged these candidate vulnerabilities:

[
  {
    "filePath": "app/closet/upload/actions.ts",
    "category": "attacker-controlled-content-type",
    "vulnerableCode": "const cutoutName = cutoutFilename(form.mediaType);\nawait supabase.storage.from(\"wardrobe\").upload(`${base}/${cutoutName}`, cut, {\n  contentType: form.mediaType,\n});",
    "explanation": "`uploadAndTag` is a Next.js server action; `form.mediaType` is a raw field from the HTTP request body. Its TypeScript type `CutoutMediaType = \"image/webp\" | \"image/png\"` is erased at runtime and there is no `zod`/schema check before use. Prior to this change, the Content-Type sent to Supabase Storage was a fixed `image/png` literal; the diff replaces that literal with `form.mediaType`, so an attacker can send any string (e.g., `text/html`, `image/svg+xml`, `application/xhtml+xml`) alongside arbitrary base64 bytes in `cutoutB64`. The stored object is then served by Supabase Storage with the attacker-chosen Content-Type. The bucket is private and RLS scopes writes to the user's own folder, so cross-tenant impact is limited, but the resulting signed URL is the exact artefact the app is designed to share (via `displayPath` \u2192 `signItemImages`); when a recipient opens it, the browser will render HTML/JS on the storage origin \u2014 enabling phishing pages, forced downloads, or SVG-with-<script> content served from a Supabase-branded URL that the victim trusts because the sender is authenticated. `cutoutFilename` bounds the extension to `.webp` / `.png`, which hides the mismatch from casual inspection but does not affect the served header. The comment claiming the stored extension and content type follow the format actually produced is only true for honest clients \u2014 the server never re-verifies.",
    "fix": "Validate `form.mediaType` at runtime against the exact allowlist before any use. For example, `const ALLOWED = new Set([\"image/webp\", \"image/png\"] as const); if (!ALLOWED.has(form.mediaType)) throw new Error(\"bad mediaType\");` (or a zod schema on the whole `form`). Additionally, prefer sniffing the actual bytes (magic-number check on `cut`) rather than trusting the client label, and set an explicit `Content-Disposition: attachment` or a fixed content type derived from server-side sniffing to defeat MIME-based phishing entirely.",
    "severity": "medium",
    "confidence": 0.85,
    "_diff_anchor": "in_diff"
  }
]

